        guide("pottery.handbuilding-methods", "Pottery", "Pinch, coil and slab handbuilding", "These three methods use different ways to distribute and join clay; consistent thickness and well-made joins reduce cracking.",
            block("Pinch form", "Start with a wedged ball and make a thumb opening while keeping a strong base.", "Rotate the piece as you pinch in small steps; support the opposite wall and check thickness regularly.", "Compress the rim and smooth or deliberately texture the surface."),
            block("Coil form", "Roll coils with an even diameter and score plus slip the contact surfaces when the clay condition requires it.", "Join each coil inside and outside so it becomes one wall; compress joins without thinning the whole wall unevenly.", "Keep the rim level and cover the work between sessions to slow drying."),
            block("Slab form", "Roll an even slab using guides or thickness rails; allow it to firm to a workable state for clean edges.", "Score and join with compatible moisture, then reinforce the inside seam without leaving a thick lump.", "Use a paper or template pattern for repeated panels and support the form while it stiffens."),
            sources = listOf("Ceramic Arts Network — handbuilding techniques" to "https://ceramicartsnetwork.org/daily/handbuilding-techniques"), related = listOf("pottery.clay-body-basics", "pottery.drying-and-cracking")),
        guide("pottery.drying-and-cracking", "Pottery", "Dry pottery evenly and diagnose cracks", "Uneven moisture loss, abrupt changes in section thickness and weak joins are common contributors to cracks.",
            block("Dry more evenly", "Keep work out of direct sun, drafts and direct heat; cover loosely so moisture can escape gradually.", "Turn or rotate pieces where appropriate so the base and walls do not dry at very different rates.", "Avoid thick bases joined to thin walls. Compress bases, rims and joins during forming.", "Let handles and attachments approach a similar moisture state to the body before joining."),
            block("Read the defect", "A crack at a join suggests insufficient scoring, slip, compression or mismatched moisture.", "A crack at the base may reflect uneven thickness, trapped stress or a base drying more slowly than the walls.", "Record where and when the crack appeared, clay body, wall thickness and drying setup; change one factor on the next test.", "Do not assume a cracked piece is safe for food use or that glaze will repair a structural crack."),
